<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siyavash</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Siyavush, Siavash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siyavash is a legendary prince of Iranian tradition, son of King Kay Kavus, whose story is told in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>. Falsely accused by his stepmother Sudabeh, he proved his innocence by passing through fire, then fled to Turan, where he was unjustly killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>, a death avenged by his son Kay Khosrow. According to al-Biruni the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> traced their line to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siyavash.jpg" alt="Siyavash" width="196" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siyavush_(The_Shahnama_of_Shah_Tahmasp).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdowsi">Ferdowsi</a>'s telling, Siyavash was born to Kay Kavus and a young woman found by Iranian knights on a hunt and kept by the Shah as his concubine. Because his mother was not of aristocratic birth, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabulistan">Zabulistan</a> to be raised by the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a>, who taught him riding, archery, hunting, conduct and speaking the truth. His name is explained as the one with black stallions, after his horse Shabrang Behzad. Returning to court, he was made ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, and his stepmother Sudabeh, daughter of the Shah of Hamavaran, tried to seduce him and proposed murdering the Shah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>, ruler of Turan, made war again, Siyavash led the Iranians against his allies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and won a crushing victory. Afrasiab sued for peace, and Siyavash agreed to hold one hundred hostages and to receive back lands the Turanians had taken, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. Kay Kavus, who had wanted Afrasiab killed, replaced him with Toos and demanded the hostages for execution. Unwilling to break the treaty and kill the hostages, Siyavash went into exile in Turan, where Afrasiab and the vizier Peeran received him warmly.</p><p>He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>'s daughter Farangis, and the couple were given Khotan, where Siyavash founded the city of Siavashgird. Jealous courtiers led by Garsivaz denounced him to Afrasiab as a traitor and persuaded Siyavash that Afrasiab meant to attack him. Afrasiab defeated his small army, but Farangis escaped with Peeran to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a> ordered Siyavash beheaded, ignoring Peeran's pleas, and where his blood fell a plant grew, later called the blood of Siavash, which ties him to the spirit of vegetative growth. In Iranian literature he is a symbol of innocence. His death is still commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> as the day of Savušun, and his story has inspired hundreds of works, among them Simin Dânešvar's novel Savušun, the first novel written in Persian by a woman. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> and Sogdia performed sacrifices and rituals dedicated to him; the historian Tolstov described him as a Central Asian god of dying and reviving vegetation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siyâvash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its stories include the romance of Zal and Rudaba, the Seven Stages (or Labours) of Rostam, Rostam and Sohrab, <b>Siyavash</b> and Sudaba, Bijan and Manijeh, the wars with Afrasiab, and Rostam and Esfandyar.</blockquote><a href="/work/shahnameh">Shahnameh · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings issued their own silver coinage, and later Khwarazmian tradition traced their line to the hero <b>Siyavush</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-khwarazm">Kingdom of Khwarazm</a></li></ul></section>
